1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,500
Hajjah Fatimah: The Widow Who
Ran a Fleet and Built a Mosque

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,153
In the early years of British
Singapore, a widow from

3
00:00:08,233 --> 00:00:12,943
Malacca ran one of the trading
businesses on the Kampong Glam shore,

4
00:00:13,023 --> 00:00:16,475
with ships of her own and
houses across the town.

5
00:00:16,475 --> 00:00:19,820
Robbers broke into her
home on Java Road twice,

6
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:24,200
and the second time they set
it on fire while she was away.

7
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:29,328
She gave thanks for her escape
by building a mosque on the site

8
00:00:29,408 --> 00:00:34,450
of her house, and in 1845 and
1846 it went up beside the sea.

9
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:38,772
The mosque still stands on Beach
Road and still bears her name,

10
00:00:38,852 --> 00:00:42,625
Hajjah Fatimah, but the sea
has long since moved away.

11
00:00:42,625 --> 00:00:46,825
Little is written down
about Fatimah's early life.

12
00:00:46,825 --> 00:00:50,158
She was Fatimah binte
Sulaiman, and the plaque

13
00:00:50,238 --> 00:00:53,725
on her mosque calls her
"a Malacca Malay lady".

14
00:00:53,725 --> 00:00:58,206
According to the Malay Heritage
Foundation, an 1834 account

15
00:00:58,286 --> 00:01:03,077
by Captain Peter Begbie names
her first husband as Rajah Moosa,

16
00:01:03,157 --> 00:01:07,329
a Bugis noble from Pulau
Penyengat in the Riau islands,

17
00:01:07,409 --> 00:01:11,275
and she separated from him
after he became insane.

18
00:01:11,275 --> 00:01:16,278
Her second husband was a Bugis
merchant of royal descent from Gowa,

19
00:01:16,358 --> 00:01:19,540
in Celebes, the island
now called Sulawesi,

20
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:24,475
which is why she is sometimes
remembered as the Sultana of Gowa.

21
00:01:24,475 --> 00:01:29,175
His business was based in Singapore,
and she moved there with him.

22
00:01:29,175 --> 00:01:33,917
In the town plan of the 1820s,
the land east of the European

23
00:01:33,997 --> 00:01:38,900
town was set aside for the Malay,
Arab and Bugis communities.

24
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:42,909
The Sultan's compound stood
at Kampong Glam, with the Arab

25
00:01:42,989 --> 00:01:47,500
quarter beside it and the Bugis
kampong further along the shore.

26
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:53,000
Beach Road was named for the obvious
reason: it ran along the beach.

27
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,125
Accounts differ on what
happened to her second husband.

28
00:01:57,125 --> 00:02:01,875
In one, he died while their
daughter, Raja Siti, was young.

29
00:02:01,875 --> 00:02:04,770
In another, he left after Raja Siti

30
00:02:04,850 --> 00:02:07,825
was born and went home to Sulawesi.

31
00:02:07,825 --> 00:02:12,650
Either way, Fatimah took over his
trading business and made it grow.

32
00:02:12,650 --> 00:02:17,506
The Malay Heritage Foundation says
the business let her own several

33
00:02:17,586 --> 00:02:22,375
shipping vessels and many properties,
in Kampong Glam and beyond.

34
00:02:22,375 --> 00:02:27,476
Trade in Singapore in the first
decades after 1819 ran largely by sea,

35
00:02:27,556 --> 00:02:31,102
between the port, the
islands of the archipelago,

36
00:02:31,182 --> 00:02:35,475
China and India, and a merchant
needed boats to take part.

37
00:02:35,475 --> 00:02:39,856
A woman running such a business
on her own account was rare in

38
00:02:39,936 --> 00:02:44,325
the colony at the time, and
Fatimah built up a large fortune.

39
00:02:44,325 --> 00:02:47,724
She also built houses for
the poor, and she made the

40
00:02:47,804 --> 00:02:51,350
pilgrimage to Mecca, which
gave her the title Hajjah.

41
00:02:51,350 --> 00:02:54,745
Her house on Java Road,
a lane in Kampong Glam

42
00:02:54,825 --> 00:02:58,225
close to the shore, was
a target for robbers.

43
00:02:58,225 --> 00:03:02,750
They broke in twice, and the
second time they set it on fire.

44
00:03:02,750 --> 00:03:07,756
The sources do not agree on when
this happened: the Malay Heritage

45
00:03:07,836 --> 00:03:13,000
Foundation places it in the 1830s,
and other accounts in the 1840s.

46
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Fatimah was away at the time and unharmed,

47
00:03:16,080 --> 00:03:19,600
and she put her escape
down to God's protection.

48
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:22,536
In thanks, she gave the
site of the house for

49
00:03:22,616 --> 00:03:25,700
a mosque and built herself
a new house nearby.

50
00:03:25,700 --> 00:03:30,062
The mosque was built in 1845
and 1846, mainly of wood,

51
00:03:30,142 --> 00:03:34,340
and for a time it was known
as the Java Road Mosque,

52
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:37,875
after the lane that has since disappeared.

53
00:03:37,875 --> 00:03:40,448
Its best-known feature is the minaret,

54
00:03:40,528 --> 00:03:45,404
which looks like a church steeple:
an octagonal tower in stacked tiers,

55
00:03:45,484 --> 00:03:48,625
rising from a square
base to a pointed spire.

56
00:03:48,625 --> 00:03:52,256
The story goes that Fatimah
hired European architects,

57
00:03:52,336 --> 00:03:55,554
and the early buildings,
the minaret among them,

58
00:03:55,634 --> 00:04:00,375
are often credited to John Turnbull
Thomson, the Government Surveyor.

59
00:04:00,375 --> 00:04:03,950
No documents have been
found to confirm it.

60
00:04:03,950 --> 00:04:08,199
Raja Siti married Syed Ahmed
Alsagoff, of a Hadhrami

61
00:04:08,279 --> 00:04:12,525
Arab merchant family that
had settled in Singapore.

62
00:04:12,525 --> 00:04:17,271
Syed Ahmed co-founded the trading
firm of Alsagoff and Company

63
00:04:17,351 --> 00:04:22,100
in 1848 and gradually took over
his mother-in-law's business.

64
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:25,621
Their son, Syed Mohamed
Alsagoff, born in 1836,

65
00:04:25,701 --> 00:04:30,064
became one of the best-known
Arab merchants and landowners

66
00:04:30,144 --> 00:04:34,048
in the colony, and after
Fatimah and Raja Siti died,

67
00:04:34,128 --> 00:04:37,575
much of her property
passed to the Alsagoffs.

68
00:04:37,575 --> 00:04:41,828
Fatimah's descendants
say she died in 1852,

69
00:04:41,908 --> 00:04:44,125
at about 98 years old.

70
00:04:44,125 --> 00:04:48,618
She was buried at her mosque, in a
mausoleum behind the prayer hall,

71
00:04:48,698 --> 00:04:51,725
and her daughter was
later buried beside her.

72
00:04:51,725 --> 00:04:55,395
Syed Ahmed is buried at
the mosque too, and a small

73
00:04:55,475 --> 00:04:59,225
cemetery for the family
lies outside the mausoleum.

74
00:04:59,225 --> 00:05:03,775
After Fatimah's death the
Alsagoffs looked after the mosque.

75
00:05:03,775 --> 00:05:08,141
Raja Siti held a feast for the
poor each year on the anniversary

76
00:05:08,221 --> 00:05:12,250
of her mother's death, and the
mosque still marks the day.

77
00:05:12,250 --> 00:05:15,700
The wooden mosque was rebuilt in stages.

78
00:05:15,700 --> 00:05:19,780
The prayer hall was rebuilt
around 1930 by the French

79
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:23,862
contractor Brossard et
Mopin, using Malay craftsmen,

80
00:05:23,942 --> 00:05:27,475
to a design by the local
firm Chung and Wong.

81
00:05:27,475 --> 00:05:32,768
The result mixes European, Moorish,
Indo-Saracenic and Chinese elements,

82
00:05:32,848 --> 00:05:37,550
and the ablution block is built
like a traditional Malay house.

83
00:05:37,550 --> 00:05:42,250
Over the years the minaret began
to tilt, as its hand-made bricks,

84
00:05:42,330 --> 00:05:46,675
weakened by damp and vibration,
shifted on the sandy ground.

85
00:05:46,675 --> 00:05:50,978
It was said to have been straightened
during repairs in the 1970s,

86
00:05:51,058 --> 00:05:53,436
but it still leans enough to be seen,

87
00:05:53,516 --> 00:05:57,700
and newspapers have called it
the "Leaning Tower of Singapore".

88
00:05:57,700 --> 00:06:01,039
When a new restoration
was planned in 2016,

89
00:06:01,119 --> 00:06:04,300
it was decided that the lean would stay.

90
00:06:04,300 --> 00:06:08,195
In the early 1970s the
streets around the mosque were

91
00:06:08,275 --> 00:06:12,475
cleared for urban renewal,
and Java Road went with them.

92
00:06:12,475 --> 00:06:16,329
The mosque was the one
building spared, and on the

93
00:06:16,409 --> 00:06:20,814
28th of June 1973 it was
gazetted as a national monument,

94
00:06:20,894 --> 00:06:24,750
in Singapore's first group
of national monuments.

95
00:06:24,750 --> 00:06:29,555
An appeal raised more than
$240,000 to repair it,

96
00:06:29,635 --> 00:06:33,025
and the work was finished in 1977.

97
00:06:33,025 --> 00:06:36,018
In 1978 it was chosen for a set of

98
00:06:36,098 --> 00:06:40,075
Singapore stamps showing
national monuments.

99
00:06:40,075 --> 00:06:44,150
It was restored again in
1998, and the soft colours

100
00:06:44,230 --> 00:06:47,979
chosen for that work won
the mosque a Singapore

101
00:06:48,059 --> 00:06:51,725
Institute of Architects
colour award in 2000.

102
00:06:51,725 --> 00:06:56,655
The mosque is now owned by the Islamic
Religious Council of Singapore,

103
00:06:56,735 --> 00:07:01,100
MUIS, and the Alsagoff family
still has a part in running it.

104
00:07:01,100 --> 00:07:04,550
As for the sea, it is now a long way off.

105
00:07:04,550 --> 00:07:07,768
Land was reclaimed along
the shore in stages,

106
00:07:07,848 --> 00:07:11,652
and Nicoll Highway, the
Marina Centre and the Kallang

107
00:07:11,732 --> 00:07:15,250
Basin now lie between
Beach Road and open water.

108
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:17,996
Even the Kallang Basin is no longer sea:

109
00:07:18,076 --> 00:07:21,953
since the Marina Barrage
closed off the bay, it has been

110
00:07:22,033 --> 00:07:25,425
part of the Marina
Reservoir, a freshwater lake.

111
00:07:25,425 --> 00:07:27,930
The map above shows roughly where the

112
00:07:28,010 --> 00:07:30,525
shore ran when the mosque was built.

113
00:07:30,525 --> 00:07:34,825
Hajjah Fatimah has been
remembered in other ways too.

114
00:07:34,825 --> 00:07:39,534
In 2014 she was one of the women
inducted into the Singapore Women's

115
00:07:39,614 --> 00:07:44,323
Hall of Fame in its first year, and
MUIS records her as one of three

116
00:07:44,403 --> 00:07:49,113
women who founded mosques in
Singapore, with Khadijah binti Mohammed

117
00:07:49,193 --> 00:07:53,700
of Masjid Khadijah and Mak Minah
Palembang of Masjid Darul Aman.

118
00:07:53,700 --> 00:07:56,544
The mosque on Beach Road was the first

119
00:07:56,624 --> 00:07:59,625
in Singapore to be named after a woman.

120
00:07:59,625 --> 00:08:03,432
Where it fits in the bigger
story: Hajjah Fatimah's story

121
00:08:03,512 --> 00:08:07,252
shows that the merchant world
of early Singapore was not

122
00:08:07,332 --> 00:08:11,412
run by men alone, and that the
Malay, Bugis and Arab families

123
00:08:11,492 --> 00:08:15,175
of Kampong Glam built much
of the town's early wealth.

124
00:08:15,175 --> 00:08:19,075
No portrait of her is known to
survive, and most of what we

125
00:08:19,155 --> 00:08:23,000
know comes from her descendants
and a few short accounts.

126
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:27,804
Her mosque, with its tilted church-like
minaret, is the record she left,

127
00:08:27,884 --> 00:08:31,275
and it marks where the
shore of the town once ran.
